WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.200
Evidence and documentation are what turn response activity into an accountable

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:08.580
investigation. During an incident, people will ask what happened, when it happened,

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.560
what systems were affected, what actions were taken, who approved them,

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:15.180
and what remains unresolved.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:20.680
If responders do not document as they work, the team may lose the timeline, repeat steps,

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.180
miss decisions, or struggle to explain the incident later.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.040
A playbook should tell responders what to record.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:33.420
At minimum, the case record should include timestamps, systems, accounts, users, alerts,

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:38.680
evidence sources, responder actions, decisions, approvals, containment steps,

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:41.460
recovery steps, and open questions.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.520
The record should distinguish between observed facts and current interpretation.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:51.100
"Observed a successful login from an unusual location" is different from "confirmed

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:56.360
account compromise." "Suspicious process detected" is different from "malware confirmed."

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:01.140
Careful language protects the integrity of the response and reduces confusion with

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:04.720
stakeholders. Evidence types vary by scenario.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:09.980
Endpoint telemetry may show processes, files, command lines, network connections,

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.080
detections, and isolation status.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:18.020
Authentication logs may show successful and failed logins, role assumptions,

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.280
token activity, new devices, or multi-factor prompts.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:26.460
Cloud audit logs may show resource creation, policy changes, key use,

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:30.280
network exposure changes, or service configuration changes.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:34.020
Email headers and message metadata can support phishing analysis.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.400
Network logs can show connection patterns.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:39.180
Screenshots may preserve a user-visible condition.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.960
Memory or disk captures may be appropriate for high-value systems when the organization

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:46.980
has trained staff and approved procedures.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.920
Evidence handling should be practical and proportional.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:52.940
Not every minor alert requires forensic imaging.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.720
But every incident needs a reliable record.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:59.540
The playbook should identify where evidence is stored, who can access it,

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:02.800
how files are named, how sensitive evidence is protected,

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:04.780
and how long records are retained.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:09.560
It should also warn responders not to alter evidence casually. For example,

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:14.180
opening a suspicious file on an uncontrolled system or forwarding sensitive data through

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.640
informal channels can create new risk.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.620
Chain of custody is the documented history of who controlled evidence,

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.280
when they received it, how it was stored, and when it was transferred.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:25.860
At a practical level,

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:30.560
it helps show that evidence was handled consistently and was not casually modified.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:34.940
Formal chain of custody may be required for legal, regulatory, employment,

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:39.400
or law enforcement matters. Even when formal custody is not required,

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:42.260
disciplined handling improves trust in the record.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.840
Good documentation also supports post-incident improvement.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:49.580
The team can see which detection worked, which evidence was missing,

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:53.240
which approval slowed containment, which communication path helped,

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.140
and which control should be improved.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:58.160
The playbook should make documentation part of response,

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:00.860
not a cleanup chore left until everyone is tired.
